    "blogtextliquid_mhjAhH": {
      "type": "editorial--blog-text-liquid",
      "custom_css": [
        "@media screen and (min-width: 1024px) {.shopify-hardcoded-text-section-content {font-size: 24px !important; margin: 0 !important; } .shopify-hardcoded-text-section-wrapper {margin-bottom: -85px; }}",
        ".shopify-hardcoded-text-section-content {font-size: 16px; margin-left: 0px; text-align: left;}",
        "@media screen and (max-width: 1024px) {.shopify-hardcoded-text-section-wrapper {min-height: 200px; padding: 16px !important; }}"
      ],
      "name": "Blog Text Liquid",
      "settings": {
        "text_content": "<p>Coachella is 95 degrees, dusty, and entirely unreasonable. The lineup is stacked, the outfits are considered well in advance, and somewhere in the middle of weekend two, someone in your group will lose a sandal in the crowd and it will not slow them down at all. This is peak American festival energy: you are doing a lot, it looks effortless, and you smell incredible while doing it.</p><p>Fragrance is not usually at the top of the packing list. Sunscreen is first. Then the portable charger, the festival outfit, the hat debate (bring the hat). But here is what makes the right scent worth adding to your bag: the Coachella heat is actually your fragrance's best asset. Warm skin amplifies everything. On a 95-degree afternoon in the Polo Fields, a good scent radiates in a way it simply does not at home in February.</p><p>There is also a practical case: bringing an inspired version of a luxury fragrance you love means you are not risking something irreplaceable in a bag that is going everywhere you go. Our bestsellers perform at the same level, cost a fraction of the price, and if the desert claims it, you just order another. Here is what to pack.</p>"
      }
    },
